<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho III of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 992–1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sancho the Great, Sancho Garcés III, Sancho III of Pamplona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho III, called the Great, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>) from about 1004 to 1035 and the most powerful ruler of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He brought Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> under his control, dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through marriage and held León for a time. His lands were divided among his sons, creating the kingdoms of Navarre, Castile and Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Sancho III of Navarre" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valdavia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lauda_Sepulcral_SanchoIII_El_Mayor_Navarra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
